BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tech-policy-press-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260924
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260925
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tech Policy Press Fellowship Program 2027
DESCRIPTION:Tech Policy Press is recruiting its 2027 fellowship cohort to p
 ursue independent reporting and analysis on urgent questions at the inters
 ection of technology and democracy. Since 2023 the programme has supported
  25 fellows from 11 countries across three cohorts\, writing on AI governa
 nce\, tech regulation\, digital rights and the entanglement of state and c
 orporate power. WHO IT IS FOR: mid-career journalists\, researchers\, lawy
 ers and public policy professionals with a strong background in a specific
  tech policy area\, a particular geography\, or technology's impact on dem
 ocracy and society. Applicants from the Global Majority and from communiti
 es underrepresented in media are encouraged\, as are projects that centre 
 communities and regions usually excluded from global tech policy conversat
 ions. Contributions can range from original reporting to policy analysis\,
  and technical practitioners are welcome as long as the work fits the publ
 ication's editorial purview. EXPECTATIONS: at least one piece of analysis 
 or reporting per month (exclusive to Tech Policy Press\, though it may be 
 republished elsewhere)\, a mandatory hour-long monthly cohort meeting\, a 
 monthly check-in with the editorial team\, collaboration with other fellow
 s and the masthead\, agreed deadlines\, and disclosure of conflicts of int
 erest. Applicants should arrive with a clear area of focus and a sense of 
 how the work will inform both the public and decision-makers. APPLY via th
 e online form by 11:59pm EDT on 24 September 2026\, with a CV or resume\, 
 a cover letter of no more than two pages\, links to prior publications (op
 en access\, published within the past three years\, at least one in Englis
 h and at least one solo-authored\; social posts\, pre-prints and unpublish
 ed coursework do not count) and two professional references. Shortlisted c
 andidates may be invited to an online interview. Decisions are expected by
  early December 2026. Two optional Zoom information sessions run on 9 Sept
 ember at 8am EDT and 16 September at 7pm EDT\; registration required.\n\nO
 rganisation: Tech Policy Press\nLocation: Fully remote\, worldwide. Intern
 ational applicants explicitly encouraged\; fellows work as independent con
 tractors. No requirement to be authorised to work in the USA.\nAward: USD 
 10\,000 stipend per fellow\, paid quarterly. Also included: editorial guid
 ance from the Tech Policy Press team\, membership of an international coho
 rt with regular feedback and exchange\, and monthly sessions to strengthen
  the work. Fellows' work is published on the Tech Policy Press website and
  promoted through the newsletter\, podcast and events. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Twelve months\, part-time\, running January to December 2027\n
 \nMore: https://www.techpolicy.press/call-for-applications-2027-tech-polic
 y-press-fellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://www.techpolicy.press/call-for-applications-2027-tech-policy-pre
 ss-fellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
LOCATION:Fully remote\, worldwide. International applicants explicitly enco
 uraged\; fellows work as independent contractors. No requirement to be aut
 horised to work in the USA.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iac-bc-ai-builders-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260821
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260822
SUMMARY:Deadline: AI Builders Fellowship 2026 (Internet Archive Canada x BC
  + AI)
DESCRIPTION:A $5\,000 fellowship to spend six weeks turning a very large Ca
 nadian public archive into something new. THE MATERIAL: Internet Archive C
 anada's Government Publications collection\, part of the Democracy's Libra
 ry project\, holding over 104\,000 text items\, roughly 22.1 million scann
 ed pages and about 48.25 TB of Canadian public-record material\, browsable
  at archive.org/details/governmentpublications. WHAT TO BUILD: deliberatel
 y open-ended. The call names a search tool that answers a real question wi
 th page-level citations\, a timeline tracing one policy across decades of 
 government language\, and a sonic or visual piece built from archival mate
 rial\, but explicitly invites work nobody has thought of yet. The requirem
 ents are that it is useful\, that it is imaginative\, and that the code is
  open source when it is done. ORIGIN: the fellowship came out of conversat
 ions between BC + AI's Kris Kruger and IAC executive director Andrea Mills
  about treating a public archive as material for new work rather than only
  something to preserve. IAC brings the archive\, BC + AI brings a communit
 y of builders used to working in public. ELIGIBILITY: anyone\, anywhere in
  the world\; one builder is selected. APPLICATION: a specific project desc
 ription grounded in the collection\, a phase-by-phase work plan for the si
 x weeks\, and a success metric\, submitted at bc-ai.ca/fellowship/apply. K
 EY DATES: applications close 21 August 2026 at 11:59pm Pacific\; fellow se
 lected by 31 August\; build runs 1 September to mid-October\; showcase at 
 Futureproof Festival in Vancouver on 28 October 2026 if the project is rea
 dy.\n\nOrganisation: Internet Archive Canada (IAC) in partnership with BC 
 + AI\nLocation: Remote\, open to builders anywhere in the world. Optional 
 in-person showcase at Futureproof Festival in Vancouver\, Canada on 28 Oct
 ober 2026 if the project is ready.\nAward: $5\,000 award for one selected 
 builder. No application fee.\nDuration: Six-week build\, 1 September to mi
 d-October 2026\, remote\, with support from BC + AI throughout\n\nMore: ht
 tps://bc-ai.ca/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
URL:https://bc-ai.ca/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, open to builders anywhere in the world. Optional in-perso
 n showcase at Futureproof Festival in Vancouver\, Canada on 28 October 202
 6 if the project is ready.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tokyo-biennale-2027-social-dive@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tokyo Biennale 2027: SOCIAL DIVE open call for artists an
 d curators
DESCRIPTION:SOCIAL DIVE is Tokyo Biennale's open call\, now in its third ed
 ition and\, for 2027\, open to both international and domestic applicants 
 (the 2019 round drew roughly 1\,500 overseas applications and the 2025 int
 ernational-only round roughly 1\,400). Artists and curators propose projec
 ts to be presented as part of Tokyo Biennale 2027\, whose theme is 'From M
 e to Us'. THEME: general producer Masato Nakamura frames the biennale agai
 nst an age of conflict and division\, arguing that to avoid repeating memo
 ries of war\, violence and exclusion we must reexamine the wisdom and cult
 ural resources passed down across generations. Tokyo carries the memories 
 of neighbourhoods dating back to the Edo period\, twice suffered great los
 s through the Great Kanto Earthquake and the Second World War\, and each t
 ime was rebuilt through the strength of its citizens\; it is also an inter
 national city where different languages\, memories\, beliefs\, wounds and 
 hopes intersect. Quoting Kenzaburo Oe\, 'I cannot live my life over again.
  But we can live our lives over again'\, the call proposes that when a sin
 gle 'me' encounters others and reconnects relationships\, a new consciousn
 ess of 'us' emerges. Art projects are treated as a form of cultural prescr
 iption\, reconnecting people with people\, people with places and people w
 ith memories. ELIGIBILITY: open to artists\, curators\, researchers\, coll
 ectives and practitioners in related fields active in Japan and abroad\; a
 pplicants must be 18 or older at the time of application\; there are no re
 strictions on nationality\, place of residence or base of operations\; par
 ticipants must be able to work in Tokyo during the designated period. ACCE
 PTED FORMATS: artwork creation\, exhibitions\, performances\, video works\
 , tours\, workshops\, participatory projects\, research projects and curat
 orial projects. Applications cover both artwork production by artists and 
 art project proposals by curators. APPLY via the Google Form linked from s
 ocialdive.jp/en (a Google account is required) during the application peri
 od 17 July to 30 September 2026\, 23:59 JST. Read the official call docume
 ntation for full eligibility requirements and guidelines.\n\nOrganisation:
  Tokyo Biennale\nLocation: Tokyo\, Japan. Selected participants carry out 
 residency-based production\, research\, meetings\, preparation and impleme
 ntation in Tokyo during a designated period. No restrictions on nationalit
 y\, place of residence or base of operations.\nAward: Travel up to JPY 300
 \,000 (economy class\, one person maximum)\, accommodation in Tokyo\, a pr
 oject development budget of up to JPY 200\,000\, and a JPY 100\,000 honora
 rium. Additional support includes venue coordination\, interpretation\, tr
 anslation and an invitation letter. No application fee.\nDuration: Residen
 cy-based work in Tokyo during a designated period\, with the project prese
 nted as part of Tokyo Biennale 2027.\n\nMore: https://socialdive.jp/en/?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://socialdive.jp/en/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Tokyo\, Japan. Selected participants carry out residency-based pro
 duction\, research\, meetings\, preparation and implementation in Tokyo du
 ring a designated period. No restrictions on nationality\, place of reside
 nce or base of operations.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:inato-gallery-touch-some-grass-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Inato Gallery: Touch Some Grass (open-call poster exhibit
 ion)
DESCRIPTION:Touch Some Grass is an open-call poster exhibition responding t
 o the growing presence of AI-generated imagery and its impact on creative 
 labour\, authorship and visual culture. At a time when images can be produ
 ced instantly and consumed just as quickly\, the exhibition reclaims the p
 oster as a space for ideas\, intention and public conversation\, inviting 
 artists to make work that questions\, challenges or reflects on this chang
 ing landscape. As the organisers put it\, the question is no longer whethe
 r machines can make images\, they can\; the question is what kind of world
  we build when speed matters more than thought. NOTE: this call offers exh
 ibition and visibility only\, with no artist fee or stipend. SUBMISSION GU
 IDELINES: artwork size A3 (297 x 420 mm)\, portrait orientation\, 300 DPI\
 , CMYK recommended\, print-quality PDF preferred\, maximum file size 1 GB\
 , one submission per artist. CAP: participation is limited to 60 artists a
 nd the submission form closes automatically once all slots are filled\, so
  the effective deadline may fall well before 31 August 2026 (10 of 60 slot
 s were taken at the time of listing). Inato Gallery is an artist-run space
  in Iligan City\, Philippines\, which began as a solo street exhibition by
  KaraTula and now runs concept-driven solo and group shows\, open mics\, t
 alks and workshops built on openness\, participation and the belief that a
 rt should exist where people already are.\n\nOrganisation: Inato Gallery\n
 Location: Submissions are digital (A3 portrait PDF) and no residency or tr
 avel is required\; the exhibition is mounted by Inato Gallery\, an artist-
 run space in Iligan City\, Philippines. No nationality restriction is stat
 ed.\nAward: No artist fee\, stipend or production budget. This is an exhib
 ition opportunity: selected posters are shown in Inato Gallery's Touch Som
 e Grass exhibition. No submission fee is stated in the call.\nDuration: On
 e-off poster exhibition. Exhibition dates are not announced yet.\n\nMore: 
 https://www.inatogallery.com/open-call?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.inatogallery.com/open-call?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Submissions are digital (A3 portrait PDF) and no residency or trav
 el is required\; the exhibition is mounted by Inato Gallery\, an artist-ru
 n space in Iligan City\, Philippines. No nationality restriction is stated
 .
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:sundance-episodic-program-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260825
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260826
SUMMARY:Deadline: Sundance Institute: Episodic Program and Lab 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Sundance Institute Episodic Program is an entry point for e
 merging to mid-career writers and creators working across all scripted ser
 ies platforms\, combining a lab experience with customised support to move
  projects and careers forward. The Episodic Lab lets writers workshop an o
 riginal pilot and develop their writing and pitching skills: working with 
 showrunners\, producers and executives\, fellows take part in one-on-one s
 tory meetings\, pitching sessions\, craft workshops and writers rooms. Up 
 to ten applicants are selected\, either for the in-person Lab (preceded by
  virtual workshops) or for a fully virtual Intensive with a creative advis
 or. ELIGIBILITY: applicants must be 18 or older\; the script must be the a
 pplicant's original creation or adapted from optioned source material and 
 written in English\; the draft must be revised or new\, not already produc
 ed\, not sold to a third party and not distributed online\; and applicants
  cannot have previously had an episodic project (pilot or pitch) produced 
 by a studio or network. Open to international and U.S. participants\, subj
 ect to travel guidelines. Up to two writers per project may participate\, 
 with one named as primary applicant. APPLICATION: submitted via FilmFreewa
 y\, including writer bios (250 words each)\, a series logline (50 words)\,
  pilot synopsis (500 words)\, one-page series overview (500 words)\, perso
 nal statement (250 words)\, community connection\, thematic statement (250
  words)\, characters and core relationships (250 words)\, episodic vision\
 , optional video samples (up to three links\, 10 minutes total)\, and the 
 full pilot script as a PDF in standard screenplay format. Demographic ques
 tions are optional and do not affect consideration. Candidates may submit 
 up to three applications\, with a separate application and USD 50 fee for 
 each project. Submissions opened 13 July 2026 and close 25 August 2026 at 
 6:00 p.m. PT. Finalists are notified by email through FilmFreeway around m
 id to late March 2027 (FilmFreeway lists 31 March 2027)\, and the list of 
 selected writers and projects is published at sundance.org/epi after 15 Ap
 ril 2027. Finalists must return a Fellow Agreement\, a Participation Agree
 ment and any other required documents within five days. Questions: episodi
 cprogram@sundance.org.\n\nOrganisation: Sundance Institute\nLocation: Open
  to international and U.S. applicants. In-person Lab takes place in the At
 lanta\, Georgia area\, USA in Spring 2027\; the Intensive track is fully o
 nline.\nAward: No stipend or honorarium for participation. For the in-pers
 on Lab\, Sundance covers round-trip basic economy airfare from the airport
  nearest the fellow's residence\, shared housing with a private room and b
 ath\, and ground transport to and from the airport (fellows within 100 mil
 es of the Lab get ground transport only). Meals\, in-room charges\, travel
  insurance\, luggage fees and taxes are the fellow's own cost. One selecte
 d project also receives a separate monetary grant from the Alfred P. Sloan
  Foundation for a series with an illuminating emphasis on science\, includ
 ing medicine\, energy\, technology or invention. APPLICATION FEE: USD 50 n
 onrefundable processing fee per project submitted.\nDuration: In-person La
 b in Spring 2027 (event dates 15 to 20 April 2027) preceded by virtual wor
 kshops\; the Intensive track convenes virtually in Summer 2027.\n\nMore: h
 ttps://filmfreeway.com/SundanceEpisodicLab2027?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://filmfreeway.com/SundanceEpisodicLab2027?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Open to international and U.S. applicants. In-person Lab takes pla
 ce in the Atlanta\, Georgia area\, USA in Spring 2027\; the Intensive trac
 k is fully online.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:sundance-collab-merrell-storytelling-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260818
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260819
SUMMARY:Deadline: Sundance Collab x Merrell: Brand-Supported Storytelling P
 rogram 2026
DESCRIPTION:Sundance Collab and Merrell are selecting five filmmakers from 
 around the world to each make an original 60 to 90 second vertical film fo
 r social platforms\, built around Merrell's 'It Starts Outside' brand plat
 form. The brief is not a commercial: it asks for a complete\, emotionally 
 resonant human story in which the outside acts as a catalyst for change\, 
 exploring how stepping outside is an antidote to increasingly screen-locke
 d\, indoor lives. Stories should reflect transformation (a moment of reset
 \, reconnection or personal shift)\, connection (belonging\, community\, r
 enewed relationship) and human truth (an observed\, emotionally grounded m
 oment). The strongest entries show what changed in a person because they w
 ent outside\, emotionally\, physically\, mentally\, creatively\, spiritual
 ly or psychologically\, rather than a final achievement. Selected filmmake
 rs enter a three-day online intensive in September on collaborating with a
  brand\, then receive USD 20\,000 to produce and deliver their greenlit co
 ncept\, cutdowns (:06\, :15\, :30) and stills between October and December
 \, with scheduled check-ins. One participant also receives a covered hotel
  stay to join the team at the Sundance Film Festival in January 2027. APPL
 ICATION: one entry per person\; all submitted materials must be created by
  the submitter\; all answers and materials must be in English or carry Eng
 lish subtitles. The core submission is a written response of up to 500 wor
 ds on how you would approach the brief. CRITICAL GUIDELINES: design for ve
 rtical with a strong hook in the first five seconds\; prioritise human\, o
 bservational storytelling over polished commercial aesthetics\; feature re
 al people and authentic moments\; treat the outside as context rather than
  the hero\; favour everyday\, intimate moments over epic outcomes\; avoid 
 overt brand narration\; no competitor products (Merrell product is provide
 d but must not be the focus)\; no AI-generated content in consumer-facing 
 materials. Selection criteria are originality\, emotional arc\, a distinct
  point of view and cultural relevance. TIMELINE: submissions opened 28 Jul
 y 2026\; deadline 11:59 p.m. PT on Tuesday 18 August 2026 (08:59 CEST on 1
 9 August)\; selected artists notified by 16 September 2026. An Insider Ses
 sion with Merrell Creative Director Alex Ho and Sundance Collab Director P
 atty West runs on 11 August. Sundance Collab provides accommodations and s
 upport services for participants with disabilities\; email accessibility@s
 undance.org to arrange services.\n\nOrganisation: Sundance Collab (Sundanc
 e Institute) in collaboration with Merrell\nLocation: Online program\, ope
 n to filmmakers worldwide. Production takes place wherever the filmmaker i
 s based. One participant receives a covered hotel stay at the Sundance Fil
 m Festival in Park City\, Utah\, USA in January 2027.\nAward: USD 20\,000 
 production budget per selected filmmaker to make and deliver the greenlit 
 concept\, cutdowns (:06\, :15\, :30) and stills. Includes an online brand-
 supported storytelling program\, and a covered hotel stay for one particip
 ant to attend the Sundance Film Festival in January 2027. No application f
 ee.\nDuration: Program runs September to December 2026\, with an online in
 tensive 30 September to 2 October 2026\, production in October\, post-prod
 uction in November and delivery by 14 December 2026.\n\nMore: https://coll
 ab.sundance.org/catalog/Merrell-Brand-Supported-Storytelling-Program?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://collab.sundance.org/catalog/Merrell-Brand-Supported-Storytellin
 g-Program?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online program\, open to filmmakers worldwide. Production takes pl
 ace wherever the filmmaker is based. One participant receives a covered ho
 tel stay at the Sundance Film Festival in Park City\, Utah\, USA in Januar
 y 2027.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:jugaad-absolute-improvisation-sac-mama-magnet-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260905
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260906
SUMMARY:Deadline: JUGAAD: Absolute Improvisation\, open call for printed ma
 tter and small-format works (Amsterdam)
DESCRIPTION:Open call from SAC Art Amsterdam and Mama Magnet for a printed 
 matter fair themed around jugaad: improvised\, resourceful solutions built
  from what is on hand (from the Sanskrit yuj\, 'to join'\, via Hindi jugar
 \, famously embodied by post-independence Punjabi vehicles pieced together
  from scrap metal and pump engines). The curators want work that engages t
 he spirit of jugaad either as subject (everyday intelligences of getting b
 y\, lateral logics of adaptation\, unlikely workarounds\, reinvention born
  of necessity) or in how it was made (found or repurposed materials\, adap
 ted processes\, structures built to solve a specific constraint). Work tha
 t came from adjustment rather than access\, problem-solving rather than bu
 dget. WHAT: zines\, artist books\, comics\, risograph or photocopied print
 s\, posters\, foldables\, instruction manuals\, packaging and other small-
 format works\; if it can travel in a suitcase it is probably in scope. No 
 framed work or anything needing gallery-standard installation. Gen-AI imag
 es and text are not accepted. WHO: artists\, designers\, writers and colle
 ctives whose practice genuinely engages with the theme\; if unsure\, reach
  out and explain in a line or two. Selected work must reach the Netherland
 s by end of September 2026. TERMS: selections made on a rolling basis 5 Au
 gust to 5 September\; selected work is sold at the fair (9-10 October\, Am
 sterdam) on mutually agreeable commission terms\, with payment within 30 d
 ays of each sale and unsold work returned after the consignment period. HO
 W TO APPLY: email info@mamamagnet.org with a CC to art@sacartamsterdam.com
 \, subject line 'JUGAAD - [Your Contribution]'\, by 5 September 2026\; ear
 lier is better given rolling selection.\n\nOrganisation: SAC Art Amsterdam
  and Mama Magnet\nLocation: Fair takes place 9-10 October 2026 in Amsterda
 m. Open internationally\, but selected work must be shipped to the Netherl
 ands by the end of September 2026 at the artist's expense.\nAward: No appl
 ication fee. Selected work is sold at the printed matter fair on mutually 
 agreeable terms regarding commission\, duration and number of editions\; a
 rtists are remunerated within 30 days of each sale. Unsold works are retur
 ned at the end of the agreed consignment period.\nDuration: Rolling select
 ion 5 August to 5 September 2026\; fair 9-10 October 2026\n\nMore: https:/
 /sacartamsterdam.com/upcoming-programmes/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://sacartamsterdam.com/upcoming-programmes/?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Fair takes place 9-10 October 2026 in Amsterdam. Open internationa
 lly\, but selected work must be shipped to the Netherlands by the end of S
 eptember 2026 at the artist's expense.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ars-biologica-artxscience-residency-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260928
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260929
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ars Biologica ArtXScience Residency 2027-2028 (Ars Electr
 onica x Ceske Budejovice ECoC 2028)
DESCRIPTION:A fully funded art-science residency from Ars Electronica and C
 eske Budejovice\, European Capital of Culture 2028\, exploring how art and
  science can collaborate on urgent environmental challenges: climate emerg
 ence\, sustainable agriculture\, geoengineering\, permaculture\, water res
 ource management\, and the broader ecological impacts on local communities
 . Selected residents work in direct collaboration with local scientists in
  Ceske Budejovice. WHO CAN APPLY: artists\, designers\, creatives and arti
 st collectives with more than 5 years of professional creative practice an
 d documented interdisciplinary work\; experience in community engagement a
 nd science collaborations is essential\; applicants must be 18+\, of any n
 ationality\, and legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. WHAT YOU G
 ET: a EUR 24\,000 package (EUR 6\,000 artist fee\, up to EUR 15\,000 produ
 ction\, up to EUR 3\,000 travel and subsistence) plus one month of housing
  covered separately\; two winners are selected. STRUCTURE: the residency r
 uns January 2027 to April 2028\, with a required on-site stay of up to one
  month in February/March 2027\, an optional stay in August/September 2027\
 , and a March 2028 stay for festival installation\, exhibition and deinsta
 llation. HOW TO APPLY: online form with project title\, a written proposal
  of max 250 words\, a scientific direction statement (max 100 words)\, art
 istic vision (max 100 words)\, community connection plan (max 100 words)\,
  a super short pitch (3 to 30 words)\, 3 to 7 images\, an optional 1-minut
 e video\, a portfolio link and contact information. Two-round selection (o
 nline application then interview)\; winners notified by November 2026. Dea
 dline 28 September 2026 at midnight CET.\n\nOrganisation: Ars Electronica 
 and Ceske Budejovice - European Capital of Culture 2028\nLocation: Ceske B
 udejovice\, Czech Republic. Open to applicants of any nationality aged 18+
  who are legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. Required on-site s
 tays: up to one month in February/March 2027\, an optional stay in August/
 September 2027\, and March 2028 for installation\, exhibition and deinstal
 lation.\nAward: EUR 24\,000 total package per winner: EUR 6\,000 artist fe
 e\, up to EUR 15\,000 production budget\, and up to EUR 3\,000 for travel\
 , accommodation and subsistence\, plus a separate budget covering one mont
 h of housing in Ceske Budejovice. Two positions available. Free to apply.\
 nDuration: January 2027 to April 2028\, with on-site blocks in early 2027 
 and March 2028\n\nMore: https://ars.electronica.art/export/en/ecoc2028-ars
 -biologica-art-science-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
URL:https://ars.electronica.art/export/en/ecoc2028-ars-biologica-art-scienc
 e-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Ceske Budejovice\, Czech Republic. Open to applicants of any natio
 nality aged 18+ who are legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. Req
 uired on-site stays: up to one month in February/March 2027\, an optional 
 stay in August/September 2027\, and March 2028 for installation\, exhibiti
 on and deinstallation.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:beinecke-short-term-fellowships-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260815
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260816
SUMMARY:Deadline: Beinecke Library Short-Term Research Fellowships 2027-202
 8 (Yale)
DESCRIPTION:The Beinecke Library Fellowship Program offers competitive shor
 t-term fellowships to support research projects that substantively engage 
 with Yale Library Special Collections materials. WHO CAN APPLY: academic a
 nd independent scholars\, locally and globally\, with or without an instit
 utional affiliation\; the library explicitly welcomes applicants regardles
 s of institutional association\, race\, cultural background\, ability\, se
 xual orientation\, gender or socioeconomic status. Applications using trad
 itional archival and bibliographic research methods are encouraged\, and s
 o are creative\, interdisciplinary and non-traditional approaches to worki
 ng with the collections\, which makes this a live option for artists and w
 riters whose practice is research-driven. WHAT YOU GET: USD 5\,000 per mon
 th for an onsite research visit of between two weeks and two months\, paid
  at the start of the fellowship whenever possible (international fellows s
 hould expect payment to take several weeks)\; fellows pay their own taxes 
 on the award. TIMELINE: applications for the 2027-2028 awards opened 1 Jun
 e 2026 and close 15 August 2026\, via Interfolio\; all decisions are notif
 ied by 31 January 2027\; research must be conducted between 1 July 2027 an
 d 30 June 2028\, and awards cannot be deferred.\n\nOrganisation: Beinecke 
 Rare Book & Manuscript Library\, Yale University\nLocation: Onsite at Yale
  Library Special Collections\, New Haven\, Connecticut\, USA. Open to acad
 emic and independent scholars locally and globally\, regardless of institu
 tional association.\nAward: USD 5\,000 per month for research visits betwe
 en two weeks and two months. Funding is paid at the beginning of the fello
 wship where possible (payment to international fellows often takes several
  weeks)\; fellows are responsible for taxes on the award. Free to apply.\n
 Duration: Two weeks to two months of onsite research\, conducted between 1
  July 2027 and 30 June 2028\; awards cannot be deferred\n\nMore: https://b
 einecke.library.yale.edu/beinecke/researchers/fellowships/short-term?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://beinecke.library.yale.edu/beinecke/researchers/fellowships/shor
 t-term?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Onsite at Yale Library Special Collections\, New Haven\, Connectic
 ut\, USA. Open to academic and independent scholars locally and globally\,
  regardless of institutional association.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:knowcase-sonar-cotec-open-call-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: KNOWCASE Open Call 2027: permanent digital-culture instal
 lation in Madrid (Sonar+D x Fundacion Cotec)
DESCRIPTION:Fundacion Cotec (a Spanish innovation-focused non-profit) and S
 onar+D\, building on an initial experiment at Sonar 2026\, invite proposal
 s for a long-term installation at KNOWCASE\, the street-level showcase of 
 Cotec's Madrid headquarters in Chamberi\, visible to the public 24 hours a
  day. THEME: digital culture\, meaning projects that combine knowledge\, r
 esearch\, science and data with art\, technology and social impact. Cotec'
 s stated priorities are the big transitions (analogue to digital\, linear 
 to circular economy\, tangible to intangible) plus innovation ecosystems a
 nd R&D&I\, the climate crisis\, the future of work and learning\, social d
 ivides\, citizen participation and democratic health. WHAT YOU GET: a budg
 et of up to EUR 100\,000 plus VAT covering everything (design\, production
 \, installation\, maintenance\, documentation\, artist fees\, travel\, acc
 ommodation and insurance)\, and a minimum 24-month public display from Feb
 ruary 2027. WHO CAN APPLY: artists\, collectives\, designers\, technologis
 ts\, universities\, research centres\, startups\, businesses\, creative ag
 encies\, producers\, cultural institutions and multidisciplinary teams\, i
 nternationally\; applicants must demonstrate solid experience and a releva
 nt portfolio. CONSTRAINTS: the proposal must be original and never previou
 sly exhibited\, must include participative or interactive elements\, must 
 encourage critical thinking\, and must be technically and financially viab
 le for the full 24+ month exhibition period (durability and maintenance ar
 e central selection criteria). Selection weighs conceptual quality highest
  (40 percent)\, followed by public interactivity (25 percent). HOW TO APPL
 Y: one PDF of maximum 15 pages containing summary\, concept\, technical pr
 oposal\, visuals\, budget\, timeline\, team profile\, portfolio and declar
 ation\, in Spanish\, another official language of Spain\, or English\, via
  the application portal linked from the terms and conditions. Results anno
 unced 14 October 2026. Deadline 7 September 2026 at midnight CEST.\n\nOrga
 nisation: Fundacion Cotec and Sonar+D\nLocation: Installation at the KNOWC
 ASE storefront of Fundacion Cotec's headquarters in Chamberi\, Madrid\, Sp
 ain\, a street-level venue visible 24/7. The call is international\; appli
 cations accepted in Spanish\, other official languages of Spain\, or Engli
 sh.\nAward: Up to EUR 100\,000 (plus VAT)\, all-inclusive: conceptualisati
 on\, design\, technical development\, production\, installation\, maintena
 nce\, documentation\, artist fees\, travel\, accommodation and insurance.\
 nDuration: Installation displayed for a minimum of 24 months\, starting Fe
 bruary 2027\n\nMore: https://sonar.es/en/news/knowcase-open-call-2027?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://sonar.es/en/news/knowcase-open-call-2027?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Installation at the KNOWCASE storefront of Fundacion Cotec's headq
 uarters in Chamberi\, Madrid\, Spain\, a street-level venue visible 24/7. 
 The call is international\; applications accepted in Spanish\, other offic
 ial languages of Spain\, or English.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:studio-alta-residence-2027-open-calls@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260910
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260911
SUMMARY:Deadline: Studio ALTA Residence 2027: four residency open calls (Pr
 ague)
DESCRIPTION:Prague performance venue Studio ALTA announces four residency o
 pen calls for 2027\, all with a single deadline of 10 September 2026. RESE
 ARCH HOUSE (19 to 28 April 2027): a shared residential laboratory for thre
 e art projects or individual artists in the early stages of exploration an
 d experimentation\, or reopening an existing finished work (performance\, 
 installation\, object\, video) to transform it into another form such as a
  workshop\, text or performance-lecture. Includes a 10-day residency\, a f
 ee of CZK 12\,000 per project\, shared studio\, individual workspace and f
 acilitated exchange meetings\; the research output must be shared publicly
  as a work-in-progress in a form based on participants' needs. REST HOUSE 
 (March to November 2027): a five-day residential spa for overloaded collea
 gues from the arts sector\, with no required output or activities\; restri
 cted to people working in production\, dramaturgy\, curation\, PR\, techni
 cal and other supporting artistic professions in the Czech independent sec
 tor. Two guests are chosen by lottery (fee CZK 6\,000 per person)\; the pu
 blic draw is announced 16 September 2026. HOUSE IN THE NEIGHBORHOOD (9 to 
 18 August 2027): for one performative art project created with the local c
 ommunity and designed or adapted for public space in Liben and its surroun
 dings\; this year's call focuses on the 55+ target group. Includes a fee o
 f CZK 30\,000 per project\, rehearsal and indoor facilities (primarily out
 door spaces)\, dramaturgic\, production\, technical and PR consultation\, 
 connections to partner organisations\, and 7 days of intensive production 
 and technical support in the final phase. A public premiere or work-in-pro
 gress in public space at the end of the residency is required. HOUSE OF SW
 EAT (1 to 10 February 2027): for one performative art project designed or 
 adapted for the specific environment of the Studio ALTA sauna and its imme
 diate surroundings\, seeking new ways of working with intimate space\, pro
 ximity to the audience and non-traditional formats. Includes a fee of CZK 
 15\,000 per project\, a 10-day residency\, sauna and rehearsal spaces\, dr
 amaturgic\, production\, technical and PR consultation\, and 2 days of int
 ensive final-phase support\; a public work-in-progress or premiere in the 
 sauna within the 9 to 12 February 2027 programme block is required. Projec
 ts at any stage of development can apply to the production calls\, and sel
 ected projects may be included in Studio ALTA's year-round programme. TIME
 LINE: deadline 10 September 2026\; results 3 weeks after the deadline (30 
 September 2026)\; a Q&A Zoom call takes place 10 August 2026 at 3:00 PM. A
 pply via the registration forms on the call page.\n\nOrganisation: Studio 
 ALTA\nLocation: Studio ALTA\, Prague (Liben district)\, Czech Republic. Th
 ree of the four calls are open internationally\; the Rest House call is re
 served for supporting-profession arts workers active in the Czech independ
 ent sector.\nAward: Residency fees paid to participants: Research House CZ
 K 12\,000 per project\; Rest House CZK 6\,000 per person\; House in the Ne
 ighborhood CZK 30\,000 per project\; House of Sweat CZK 15\,000 per projec
 t. All include workspace at Studio ALTA\; the two production residencies a
 dd dramaturgic\, production\, technical and PR support. Free to apply.\nDu
 ration: Programme dependent: 5 to 10 days\, at various points between Febr
 uary and November 2027\n\nMore: https://www.altart.cz/rezidence-2027/?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.altart.cz/rezidence-2027/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Studio ALTA\, Prague (Liben district)\, Czech Republic. Three of t
 he four calls are open internationally\; the Rest House call is reserved f
 or supporting-profession arts workers active in the Czech independent sect
 or.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tech4dev-ai-cohort-program-3-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260810
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260811
SUMMARY:Deadline: Project Tech4Dev: AI Cohort Program 3.0 (NGOs building AI
  solutions)
DESCRIPTION:The third round of Project Tech4Dev's AI Cohort\, a hands-on im
 plementation programme for NGOs designing\, building and deploying respons
 ible AI solutions inside their own programmes. Cohort 2.0 worked with seve
 n NGOs across education\, civic engagement\, youth development and nonprof
 it technology\, who built and piloted AI teaching assistants\, volunteer c
 oaches\, mentoring tools and decision-support systems\; the organisers' st
 ated lesson was that adoption turns on understanding user needs\, testing 
 rapidly\, measuring impact and building responsibly rather than on the tec
 hnology itself. Cohort 3.0 is aimed at organisations ready to move past ex
 ploration into solutions that can be deployed in real-world settings. STRU
 CTURE (three sprints over roughly six months): Discovery Sprint (months 1 
 to 2) covering problem discovery\, use-case prioritisation\, data-readines
 s assessment\, user research and solution design\; Build Sprint (months 2 
 to 5) covering prototype development\, implementation sprints\, user testi
 ng\, mentorship and responsible AI evaluations\; and Activation Sprint (mo
 nth 6) covering pilot deployment\, impact assessment\, operationalisation 
 planning and a final showcase with peers and ecosystem partners. SELECTION
  CRITERIA: NGOs must show some in-house technical or data capability\, ide
 ally a dedicated person who can anchor the work (not necessarily an AI exp
 ert\, but able to manage workflows\, communicate operational requirements 
 and work with mentors and experts)\; must commit one person's full bandwid
 th for the whole programme\, including aligning leadership\, adapting oper
 ations and making room to test\, monitor and refine\; and preference goes 
 to problems involving multi-modal input\, especially Indic languages\, and
  to use cases built on LLMs. Applications are open to nonprofits across se
 ctors\, with priority to climate-focused organisations whose AI use cases 
 could improve programme delivery\, decision-making or community outcomes. 
 Apply via the Google Form linked from the announcement before 10 August 20
 26.\n\nOrganisation: Project Tech4Dev\nLocation: Remote cohort programme. 
 No geographic restriction is stated in the call\, but the framing is India
 -centred: preference goes to problems involving multi-modal input and Indi
 c languages.\nAward: FLAG\, UNVERIFIED: no grant amount\, stipend\, fee or
  compute credit is stated. What the call describes is hands-on implementat
 ion support\, mentorship and expert guidance across three sprints\; partic
 ipating NGOs must invest one person's full bandwidth for the duration.\nDu
 ration: Six months\, tentatively mid-September 2026 to January or February
  2027 (subject to change)\n\nMore: https://projecttech4dev.org/ai-cohort-p
 rogram-3-0-empowering-ngos-to-scale-impact-with-ai/?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://projecttech4dev.org/ai-cohort-program-3-0-empowering-ngos-to-sc
 ale-impact-with-ai/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refer
 ral
LOCATION:Remote cohort programme. No geographic restriction is stated in th
 e call\, but the framing is India-centred: preference goes to problems inv
 olving multi-modal input and Indic languages.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:resilient-futures-ideation-grants-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260904
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260905
SUMMARY:Deadline: Resilient Futures Call 2026: Ideation Grants (climate res
 ilience and political leadership)
DESCRIPTION:NOTE: this is a climate-and-democracy funder rather than an art
 s or technology programme\; relevance to creative or tech practice depends
  on whether political leadership is a genuine driver of the proposed model
 . The Resilient Futures Call is a co-funded initiative by the Better Polit
 ics Fund\, the European Climate Foundation\, Naia Trust and the Vibe Fund 
 supporting non-partisan initiatives working at the intersection of democra
 tic political leadership and climate resilience globally. Total available 
 funding is USD 700\,000 across two streams: Ideation Grants (open call\, t
 his entry) and Incubation Grants (nomination only\, up to USD 50\,000\, fo
 r approaches already proven beyond early testing\; nominees are contacted 
 directly and there is no way to apply). WHAT IS FUNDED\, across three area
 s: (1) Pathways\, helping climate scientists\, advocates and affected comm
 unities work more effectively with political and governance decision-maker
 s so that evidence and local knowledge shape policy and policy is carried 
 through at every level of government\, from town councils to national parl
 iaments\; (2) Leadership\, programs integrating a critical climate lens in
 to democratic leadership development\, strengthening ethical communication
 \, policy design\, implementation and strategic coalition-building\, inclu
 ding pipelines for leaders from underrepresented backgrounds\; (3) Cross-S
 ector Leadership\, backing leadership on energy\, housing\, transport\, fo
 od\, security\, health and conservation\, and helping reform political sys
 tems and institutions so leaders and new ideas in these areas can succeed.
  The funders want work where political leadership is a key driver of chang
 e\, not a side effect. WHO CAN APPLY: organizations\, networks\, collectiv
 es or individuals anywhere eligible. No need to be a registered charity an
 d no need to be based in a priority country\, though it strengthens region
 al fit. ELIGIBILITY CHECK (all must be yes): the idea is new or early-stag
 e (organization under 12 months old\, or a new pilot from an older organiz
 ation\; established programs with a strong track record are not right for 
 an Ideation Grant)\; it aligns with at least one of the three funding area
 s and with regional priorities\; it is non-partisan (no parties\, candidat
 es or campaigns)\; the applicant is based in an eligible location. PRIORIT
 Y COUNTRIES: Africa (Kenya\, Nigeria\, South Africa)\; Asia (Bangladesh\, 
 Indonesia\, Nepal\, Philippines\, Sri Lanka\, Thailand)\; Europe (France\,
  Germany\, Hungary\, Italy\, Poland\, Romania\, Spain\, UK)\; Latin Americ
 a (Argentina\, Bolivia\, Colombia\, Ecuador\, Guyana\, Peru\, Suriname)\; 
 Pacific (Cook Islands\, Federated States of Micronesia\, Fiji\, Kiribati\,
  Marshall Islands\, Nauru\, Niue\, Palau\, Papua New Guinea\, Samoa\, Solo
 mon Islands\, Timor-Leste\, Tonga\, Tuvalu\, Vanuatu). CANNOT FUND\, for l
 egal and operational reasons\, individuals and organizations in: Afghanist
 an\, Azerbaijan\, Bahrain\, Belarus\, Burkina Faso\, China\, Cuba\, DR Con
 go\, Eritrea\, Eswatini\, Georgia\, Guinea\, Guinea-Bissau\, Haiti\, India
 \, Iran\, Jordan\, Kuwait\, Laos\, Libya\, Mali\, Morocco\, Myanmar/Burma\
 , Nicaragua\, Niger\, North Korea\, Oman\, Palestine (Gaza)\, Pakistan\, Q
 atar\, Russia\, Saudi Arabia\, Syria\, Sudan\, South Sudan\, Somalia\, Tur
 kmenistan\, Uganda\, United Arab Emirates\, the United States\, Uzbekistan
 \, Vietnam\, Yemen\, and Russian-occupied territories in Ukraine (Crimea\,
  Donetsk\, Luhansk\, Zaporizhzhia\, Kherson). NOT FUNDED: physical infrast
 ructure such as solar panels or climate infrastructure where political lea
 dership is not a driver of the model\; research\, accountability scorecard
 s and policy-gap mapping (the funders support the leaders and institutions
  who act on evidence\, not the generation of it)\; public awareness campai
 gns without a political leadership dimension\; anything affiliated with a 
 political party or candidate\; corporate sustainability or ESG initiatives
 \; outside-pressure advocacy campaigns that push governments on emissions 
 targets without building new leadership capacity\; citizens' assemblies or
  deliberation projects with no link to political leaders or institutions a
 cting on the outcomes\; transparency or open-data initiatives that do not 
 feed into political or institutional decision-making\; watchdog or investi
 gative journalism without a direct link to strengthening political leaders
 hip or institutional response. ASSESSMENT CRITERIA: innovation (a genuinel
 y new idea\, method or audience)\; impact potential\; strategic clarity (a
  credible plan connecting activities to the intended outcome)\; financial 
 clarity\; regional fit\; leadership (motivation\, experience and rootednes
 s in local context). PROCESS: fill out the short application form before m
 idnight CET on 4 September 2026. There is no second full proposal stage. E
 very application is acknowledged\; all are screened and shortlisted\, and 
 a Grantmaking Council of eight regional experts across Asia\, Europe\, Lat
 in America\, Africa and the Pacific reviews the shortlist and makes recomm
 endations. Shortlisted applicants are asked for supporting documentation f
 or due diligence\, sometimes including a short call. Feedback is only give
 n to shortlisted applicants\; no reply by December 2026 means the applicat
 ion was unsuccessful. INFORMATION SESSION: online 17 August 2026\, two sit
 tings at 8:00 CET and 16:00 CET\, registration on the call page. Questions
 : resilientfutures@betterpolitics.foundation.\n\nOrganisation: Better Poli
 tics Fund\, European Climate Foundation\, Naia Trust and the Vibe Fund\nLo
 cation: Global\, with priority countries per region. Cannot fund applicant
 s in a long list of excluded countries\, which includes the United States\
 , India\, Pakistan\, China\, Russia and others (full list on the call page
 ).\nAward: USD 700\,000 total across the whole call (Ideation plus Incubat
 ion streams). Ideation Grants: USD 10\,000 to USD 20\,000. Free to apply. 
 Incubation Grants (up to USD 50\,000) are by nomination only and have no a
 pplication process.\nDuration: Not specified\; grants support new or early
 -stage ideas and pilots. Decisions announced late November / early Decembe
 r 2026.\n\nMore: https://www.betterpolitics.foundation/resilient-futures-c
 all-2026?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.betterpolitics.foundation/resilient-futures-call-2026?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Global\, with priority countries per region. Cannot fund applicant
 s in a long list of excluded countries\, which includes the United States\
 , India\, Pakistan\, China\, Russia and others (full list on the call page
 ).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:instituto-sacatar-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: Instituto Sacatar: Sacatar Fellowship 2027 (Bahia\, Brazi
 l)
DESCRIPTION:Brazil's longest-running artist residency\, on the island of It
 aparica across the bay from Salvador\, hosting six to eight artists per se
 ssion for roughly eight weeks. Open to all creative disciplines and\, unus
 ually for this region\, to all nationalities\, which makes it one of the f
 ew genuinely international residencies in Latin America. Sacatar provides 
 full hosting (private room\, three meals daily\, individual studio\, laund
 ry\, local logistics and connections) but explicitly does NOT provide a st
 ipend\, airfare to Salvador\, art materials or discretionary local expense
 s\, so applicants must fund their own travel. Individual artists only\; gr
 oup proposals are not accepted and former Sacatar Fellows may not reapply.
  COVID vaccination is required. NOTE: there is a USD 25 application fee. A
 pply through SlideRoom by 28 August 2026. Contact info@sacatar.org.\n\nOrg
 anisation: Instituto Sacatar\nLocation: Itaparica island\, Bahia\, Brazil.
  Open to all nationalities.\nAward: Fully hosted residency: private bedroo
 m with attached bathroom\, three meals a day (except Saturday evenings\, S
 undays and Brazilian holidays)\, an individual studio suited to the discip
 line\, weekly laundry\, logistical support and airport transfer from Salva
 dor. NO STIPEND\, no airfare and no materials budget. USD 25 application f
 ee. One French artist may additionally receive airfare and a stipend throu
 gh Villa Fatumbi.\nDuration: Approximately eight weeks\; specific session 
 dates are listed in the application.\n\nMore: https://sacatar.org/en/fello
 wships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://sacatar.org/en/fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Itaparica island\, Bahia\, Brazil. Open to all nationalities.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ace-pice-movilidad-2026-fase3@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: Accion Cultural Espanola: PICE Movilidad 2026 (Phase 3)
DESCRIPTION:PICE Movilidad is AC/E's collaboration scheme with foreign cult
 ural institutions: a festival\, biennial\, museum\, venue\, gallery or pla
 tform OUTSIDE Spain applies for AC/E to cover the costs of programming Spa
 nish or Spain-resident artists\, collectives and cultural professionals in
  its own public programme. This is one of the clearest routes for a non-Sp
 anish festival to have a foreign national fund pay for part of its program
 me. Ten thematic areas are covered including Artes visuales\, Musica y cre
 acion sonora\, Podcast\, Videojuego y animacion and Arquitectura y diseno\
 , so tech-crossover\, sound-art and media-art programming is squarely in s
 cope. For visual arts and architecture/design\, covered costs include prod
 uction materials\, purchase or rental of technical equipment\, hiring of t
 echnicians\, curators\, coordinators and designers\, reproduction and exhi
 bition rights\, logistics and transport of works\, publications\, document
 ation\, accommodation\, per diems\, travel and insurance\; for Musica y cr
 eacion sonora coverage is narrower (travel\, accommodation\, per diems\, i
 nternal transport). Non-EU and UK organisations are fully eligible\; there
  is no EU-membership requirement. Natural persons are barred. TIMING: Phas
 e 3 closes 7 September 2026\, provisional list 21 October\, definitive awa
 rd list 30 October\, and Phase 3 projects may not begin before 1 November 
 2026\, so this window cannot retro-fund an event already scheduled for aut
 umn 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Accion Cultural Espanola (AC/E)\nLocation: Anyw
 here outside Spain\, at the applicant's own venue or event. Applicant must
  be a foreign entity constituted outside Spain.\nAward: Max EUR 15\,000 pe
 r applicant per call\, per event and per thematic area\; within that\, max
  EUR 5\,000 per participant or collective. Total 2026 call budget EUR 1\,8
 10\,000 (Musica y creacion sonora EUR 320\,000\, Artes visuales EUR 200\,0
 00\, Videojuego y animacion EUR 90\,000\, Arquitectura y diseno EUR 90\,00
 0\, Podcast EUR 90\,000). Mandatory co-financing: the applicant must commi
 t at least 50 percent of each proposal's total budget. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Single event or project\; Phase 3 activities must start on or 
 after 1 November 2026 and finish before 28 February 2027.\n\nMore: https:/
 /www.accioncultural.es/es/movilidad-2026?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.accioncultural.es/es/movilidad-2026?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Anywhere outside Spain\, at the applicant's own venue or event. Ap
 plicant must be a foreign entity constituted outside Spain.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:statens-kunstfond-production-exhibitions-2026@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260924
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260925
SUMMARY:Deadline: Statens Kunstfond: Production and Exhibitions\, Contempor
 ary Art in Denmark and Abroad (2026 autumn round)
DESCRIPTION:The Danish Arts Foundation's Production and Exhibitions program
 me funds the production\, dissemination and exhibition of contemporary art
  in Denmark and abroad\, and it is one of the few Nordic national schemes 
 a foreign organisation can apply to directly. Eligible costs include creat
 ing new works where an exhibition agreement is in place\, exhibition costs
 \, exhibition programming featuring Danish or international artists\, prom
 otion tied to an exhibition\, and honorariums covering artists' work and i
 nstallation time\, which lets a festival or biennial cover commission and 
 artist-fee lines. THE KEY CONDITION for non-Danish applicants is that the 
 exhibition must feature Danish artists\; Danish artists and venues face no
  such restriction. No minimum or maximum grant is published since the comm
 ittee decides case by case\, and previous award levels are visible on the 
 site. Two 2026 deadlines apply: 26 March 2026 (closed) and 24 September 20
 26 at 14:00\, with the application form opening one month before each dead
 line. TIMING: applicants receive a response no later than twelve weeks aft
 er the deadline\, so expect a decision by mid-December 2026. Applications 
 must go through the Danish digital subsidy portal\; submissions outside it
  are rejected except in very special circumstances. FLAG: earliest-start-d
 ate rules are not specified on the funder's page.\n\nOrganisation: Statens
  Kunstfond (Danish Arts Foundation)\nLocation: Denmark and abroad. Non-Dan
 ish venues and organisations may apply\, but only for exhibitions that fea
 ture Danish artists.\nAward: DKK. No published minimum or maximum\; the co
 mmittee assesses each application on its merits and historical awards are 
 published on the site. No stated co-financing requirement. No application 
 fee. Applications must be submitted digitally via the Danish subsidy porta
 l (Tilskudsportalen).\nDuration: Project-length\; production and exhibitio
 n periods vary.\n\nMore: https://www.kunst.dk/english/funding-programmes/e
 xhibition-of-danish-art-abroad?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://www.kunst.dk/english/funding-programmes/exhibition-of-danish-ar
 t-abroad?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Denmark and abroad. Non-Danish venues and organisations may apply\
 , but only for exhibitions that feature Danish artists.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kulturstiftung-des-bundes-general-project-funding-2026@artificialnouvea
 u.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: Kulturstiftung des Bundes: General Project Funding (Allge
 meine Projektfoerderung)
DESCRIPTION:Germany's flagship open\, non-thematic federal project line\, a
 nd one of the largest European funders that will back a festival\, exhibit
 ion programme or multi-partner convening run by an organisation rather tha
 n an individual artist. It funds large innovative projects across all arti
 stic fields\, explicitly including New Media alongside visual and performi
 ng arts\, music\, film\, photography and architecture. There is no publish
 ed maximum\, but the EUR 50\,000 minimum total project cost sets the scale
 \, and at least 20 percent co-financing is mandatory. Deadlines are twice 
 yearly at 12:00 CET: 31 July 2026 and 31 January 2027. TIMING RULE: decisi
 ons are made by an external jury meeting in May and November\, and no proj
 ect may start before its jury date\, so plan four to six months of lead ti
 me. Non-German organisations are genuinely eligible\, which is unusual at 
 this scale\, provided the project is visible in Germany and has an interna
 tional dimension. Two practical barriers: the application form must be com
 pleted in German\, and project communications must include a German-langua
 ge version.\n\nOrganisation: Kulturstiftung des Bundes (German Federal Cul
 tural Foundation)\nLocation: Activity must be visible to audiences in Germ
 any. Institutions headquartered in Germany or abroad may apply.\nAward: No
  fixed ceiling\, but total project costs must exceed EUR 50\,000 to be eli
 gible. The Foundation allocates up to EUR 10 million per year across this 
 line. At least 20 percent of total project costs must be secured from own 
 capital and/or third-party funding. No application fee.\nDuration: Project
 -length. Projects may not begin before the jury meeting.\n\nMore: https://
 www.kulturstiftung-des-bundes.de/en/themes_focuses/general_project_funding
 .html?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.kulturstiftung-des-bundes.de/en/themes_focuses/general_proj
 ect_funding.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Activity must be visible to audiences in Germany. Institutions hea
 dquartered in Germany or abroad may apply.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:terra-foundation-collections-grants-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260803
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260804
SUMMARY:Deadline: Terra Foundation for American Art: Collections Grants (20
 26 cycle)
DESCRIPTION:Funds organisations to reinstall permanent collections or mount
  temporary exhibitions drawn primarily from their own holdings\, on condit
 ion the project broadens understandings of American art and pursues inclus
 ive\, expansive curatorial practice. Planning grants of USD 25\,000 to 75\
 ,000 cover research\, advisory committees and conservation\; implementatio
 n grants up to USD 100\,000 cover interpretation\, exhibition design\, con
 servation\, public programmes and dissemination of research\, so the publi
 c-programming layer of an exhibition is fundable here. TIMING: the 2026 cy
 cle is two-stage\, with a grant inquiry due 3 August 2026\, a full proposa
 l for shortlisted applicants due 13 October 2026\, external panel review\,
  decisions in April 2027 and projects starting after 1 May 2027. That is a
  nine-month decision lag\, so this is planning-horizon money. Internationa
 l applicants are explicitly welcomed and need US 501(c)(3) status or the i
 nternational equivalent. The Foundation will not fund acquisitions\, equip
 ment\, exclusively online projects\, or projects consisting primarily of l
 oans. The American-art subject requirement is the main constraint for medi
 a-arts organisations.\n\nOrganisation: Terra Foundation for American Art\n
 Location: Worldwide. Museums\, art centres and community-based cultural or
 ganisations both inside and outside the US.\nAward: Planning grants USD 25
 \,000 to 75\,000 (conservation capped at 25 percent of a planning award)\;
  implementation grants up to USD 100\,000. No formal match\, but the Found
 ation prefers not to be sole funder. No application fee.\nDuration: Projec
 t-based\; funded projects start after 1 May 2027 following April 2027 deci
 sions.\n\nMore: https://www.terraamericanart.org/what-we-offer/grant-progr
 am/collections-grants/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
URL:https://www.terraamericanart.org/what-we-offer/grant-program/collection
 s-grants/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Worldwide. Museums\, art centres and community-based cultural orga
 nisations both inside and outside the US.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:terra-foundation-convening-grants-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260928
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260929
SUMMARY:Deadline: Terra Foundation for American Art: Convening Grants (Roun
 d 1\, 2026-27)
DESCRIPTION:The most directly relevant Terra line for organisations running
  convenings rather than exhibitions: conversations\, symposia and workshop
 s that stimulate interdisciplinary and intercultural exchange. Covered cos
 ts are unusually broad and practical\, including professional fees\, artis
 t honoraria\, travel\, AV support\, interpretation and translation\, cater
 ing\, research dissemination\, marketing\, and administrative costs up to 
 20 percent. Awards run USD 10\,000 to 25\,000 and are made twice yearly. T
 he process is two-stage: Round 1 has a grant inquiry due 28 September 2026
  followed\, if invited\, by a full proposal due 30 November 2026\, for pro
 grammes taking place after 1 May 2027. Round 2 has an inquiry due 16 Febru
 ary 2027 and proposal due 20 April 2027 for programmes after 1 November 20
 27. Invited proposals go to an external panel of curators and arts profess
 ionals. International applicants are explicitly eligible and need US 501(c
 )(3) status or a recognised non-profit equivalent in their own country. Th
 e binding constraint is topical rather than geographic: a European or Asia
 n organisation can win this\, but the convening must engage American or In
 digenous North American visual art\, which is a workable frame for art-and
 -technology or sound-art programming with a US axis.\n\nOrganisation: Terr
 a Foundation for American Art\nLocation: Worldwide. Open to organisations 
 anywhere\, but subject matter must relate to the visual arts of the United
  States or Indigenous arts of North America.\nAward: USD 10\,000 to 25\,00
 0. Administrative costs capped at 20 percent of the grant. No formal match
  required\, but the Foundation prefers not to be sole funder. No applicati
 on fee.\nDuration: Event-based\; Round 1 supports programmes taking place 
 after 1 May 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.terraamericanart.org/what-we-offer/
 grant-program/convening-grants/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_
 medium=referral
URL:https://www.terraamericanart.org/what-we-offer/grant-program/convening-
 grants/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Worldwide. Open to organisations anywhere\, but subject matter mus
 t relate to the visual arts of the United States or Indigenous arts of Nor
 th America.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:japan-foundation-exhibitions-abroad-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261201
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261202
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Japan Foundation: Exhibitions Abroad Support Program 
 (FY2027-28 round)
DESCRIPTION:The Japan Foundation's flagship grant for organisations abroad 
 mounting an exhibition introducing Japanese art and culture\, explicitly c
 overing overseas biennials and triennials that present Japanese artists. I
 t is a partial-cost grant rather than a production budget: it pays toward 
 shipping Japanese works\, producing print or digital catalogues\, and brin
 ging Japanese artists\, curators and specialists to the venue. That cost s
 tructure suits media arts\, digital culture and art-and-science exhibition
 s well\, where shipping technical work and hosting the artist for installa
 tion are the main cost drivers. It is explicitly designed for non-Japanese
  applicants: museums\, galleries and comparable organisations outside Japa
 n. Institutions only\, so independent curators and artists cannot apply di
 rectly and must be hosted by an eligible institution. Exhibition content m
 ust introduce Japanese art and culture\, so a Japanese artistic component 
 is mandatory. Recent UK recipients include Tate Modern\, the V&A and the H
 ayward Gallery\, so the scheme skews toward established institutions. Appl
 icants are asked to discuss eligibility with the relevant regional arts pr
 ogramme officer before applying. FLAG: full FY2027 guidelines were not yet
  published by Tokyo headquarters as of July 2026 and this deadline is the 
 funder's own provisional date\; reconfirm in autumn 2026.\n\nOrganisation:
  The Japan Foundation\nLocation: Exhibition takes place at the applicant i
 nstitution's own venue outside Japan. National rounds run through Japan Fo
 undation offices worldwide.\nAward: No published ceiling. Partial-cost gra
 nt only: packing and shipping of Japanese exhibits (insurance excluded)\, 
 catalogue production including digital catalogues\, and travel for invited
  Japanese artists\, curators and specialists. Preliminary research and dev
 elopment explicitly excluded. The grant is partial by design\, so the host
  institution funds the balance. No application fee.\nDuration: Exhibitions
  must open between 1 April 2027 and 31 March 2028\; results notified April
  2027.\n\nMore: https://www.jpf.org.uk/artsandculture/funding.php?utm_sour
 ce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.jpf.org.uk/artsandculture/funding.php?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Exhibition takes place at the applicant institution's own venue ou
 tside Japan. National rounds run through Japan Foundation offices worldwid
 e.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:project-liberty-co-lab-fund-round-2-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260729
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260730
SUMMARY:Deadline: Project Liberty: Co-Lab Fund Micro-Grants (Round 2\, 2026
 )
DESCRIPTION:MEMBERS ONLY: this is a member-led collaboration fund\, not an 
 open public call. Projects must be led by a Project Liberty Coalition (All
 iance) member organization. Round 2 shifts to micro-grants of USD 10\,000 
 or less\, an iterative change from Round 1 intended to spread support acro
 ss more of the Coalition\; organizations that did not receive a Round 1 aw
 ard are preferred. SCOPE: micro-grants fund two kinds of work\, (1) discre
 te projects that strengthen the Coalition itself\, such as Shared Services
  or convenings\, and (2) time-sensitive collaborative projects within a de
 signated workstream. In both cases the work should begin quickly and produ
 ce something concrete. A STRONG APPLICATION SHOWS: work that builds the Co
 alition as an entity\, such as shared tools\, infrastructure or connection
 s that make joint action easier\; clear alignment with a Coalition workstr
 eam\; value for multiple Coalition organizations\; a strong case that the 
 work would not happen\, or would move more slowly\, without the Fund\; a c
 oncrete outcome such as a tool\, resource\, convening\, campaign or resear
 ch product\; and readiness to begin substantive work within two weeks of a
 pproval. LIMIT: one application per organization\, submitted through the e
 xisting Co-Lab Fund application form. Round 1 results were still pending a
 t the time of this call\, with funded projects to be shared soon and engag
 ement opportunities expected across the work. Prospective applicants unsur
 e whether an idea fits this round are invited to reach out first. DEADLINE
 : 29 July 2026 at midnight. Background on the Co-Lab program is in the Coa
 lition's overview document.\n\nOrganisation: Project Liberty (Alliance Ste
 erCo)\; pooled fund supported by Future of Life Institute\, Omidyar Networ
 k\, Ergo Impact\, and Project Liberty\nLocation: Members of the Project Li
 berty coalition (Alliance). Projects led by a Coalition member organizatio
 n\; geography otherwise unrestricted.\nAward: Micro-grants of USD 10\,000 
 or less\, a deliberate change from Round 1's larger awards so the Fund rea
 ches more of the Coalition. Preference given to organizations that did not
  receive a Round 1 award. No application fee.\nDuration: Short\, discrete 
 projects. Applicants must be ready to begin substantive work within two we
 eks of approval. This is the final round of the summer\; the Fund is expec
 ted to resume later in 2026.\n\nMore: https://forms.fillout.com/t/6AGEGhTk
 NJus?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://forms.fillout.com/t/6AGEGhTkNJus?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Members of the Project Liberty coalition (Alliance). Projects led 
 by a Coalition member organization\; geography otherwise unrestricted.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:antikythera-agentworld-journal-cfp-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260820
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260821
SUMMARY:Deadline: Antikythera Journal: Agentworld Special Issue\, Call for 
 Papers
DESCRIPTION:Antikythera\, a research programme on planetary computation and
  the evolution of intelligence\, invites contributions to Agentworld\, a s
 pecial issue of its peer-reviewed MIT Press journal launching Fall 2026. T
 HEME: Agentworld is a research initiative on the medium-term future of hyb
 rid human-AI societies\, framed not around tools for agentic task completi
 on but around what the brief calls 'the human perplexity of deep agentic d
 iffusion'. It treats human societies as the evolutionary niches into which
  new artificial species must adapt\, where the constraints of agentic inte
 gration are qualitative and open-ended (psychological\, philosophical\, li
 nguistic\, symbolic\, existential) rather than utility functions to be sol
 ved. The framing describes a 'parasociety' of densely reticulated social g
 raphs in which agent-to-agent communication constitutes most socially sign
 ificant interaction\, humans and agents anthropomorphise each other by inh
 eritance rather than necessity\, and the two overlapping societies act as 
 mutual evolutionary niches. The disciplinary posture proposed is 'a preemp
 tive anthropology of open-world centaur societies': building the vocabular
 y\, scenarios and conceptual armature for an emergent sociality while it i
 s still forming\, rather than describing it after the fact. WHAT THEY WANT
 : theoretical essays and technical papers that engage the Agentworld theme
 s directly and explain how the contribution adds to the special issue's co
 re questions. Philosophical\, theoretical\, technical\, design-led and pro
 duct-oriented research are all in scope. The call explicitly favours work 
 that exceeds conventional academic journal formats\, including contributio
 ns combining philosophical argument\, technical analysis\, design research
 \, diagrams\, software\, infrastructure proposals\, visual media or web-na
 tive formats. Contributors may optionally propose still images\, diagrams\
 , video\, web-based or interactive components\, and should say whether the
 y have the design\, development or production capacity to realise them\; t
 hese elements are not required. SUBMIT: an abstract or proposal of up to 5
 00 words\; author name(s)\, affiliation(s)\, contact details\, biography(i
 es) and CV(s)\; a designated lead author or contact person for collaborati
 ve submissions\; and a portfolio with at least one relevant work sample su
 ch as a preprint\, published article or paper. Full paper drafts are encou
 raged where available but not required at the initial stage. Submissions m
 ay be individual or collaborative. TIMING: the review window opened 25 Jun
 e 2026. NOTE: no closing date is published on the call page\; the deadline
  shown here is a placeholder set roughly a month out\, so check the site o
 r submit early.\n\nOrganisation: Antikythera (Antikythera: Journal for the
  Philosophy of Planetary Computation\, published with MIT Press)\nLocation
 : Remote (online submission\; the journal is web-first and design-forward)
 \nAward: Unpaid. No honorarium or fee is offered for accepted contribution
 s\; the return is peer-reviewed publication in the MIT Press-published Ant
 ikythera Journal. No submission fee.\nDuration: Single publication\; first
  selection of articles published Fall 2026\n\nMore: https://agentworld.ant
 ikythera.org/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral#ca
 ll-for-papers
URL:https://agentworld.antikythera.org/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral#call-for-papers
LOCATION:Remote (online submission\; the journal is web-first and design-fo
 rward)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:govai-research-fellow-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260816
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260817
SUMMARY:Deadline: GovAI Research Fellows and Research Scholars (AI Governan
 ce) 2026
DESCRIPTION:GovAI is hiring for two research roles with a single shared app
 lication (apply once to be considered for both). RESEARCH FELLOWS play a c
 entral role in GovAI's research: experienced researchers who conduct indep
 endent\, high-quality work to inform high-stakes decisions in government a
 nd industry\, whether through policy memos\, blog posts\, academic publica
 tions or strategic advising\, alongside mentoring early-career researchers
  (such as Summer and Winter Fellows) and helping shape GovAI's research ag
 enda. Two-year contract with the possibility of renewal\; Fellows work und
 er a Senior Research Fellow or leadership-team member but have substantial
  autonomy over projects\, agendas and publication formats. RESEARCH SCHOLA
 RS join for a one-year visiting position designed to support the career de
 velopment of AI governance researchers and practitioners: conducting polic
 y\, social science or technical research\, advising policymakers\, or star
 ting new applied projects. TOPICS include risk management\, threat modelin
 g\, economics of AI\, geopolitics of AI\, public policy and technical gove
 rnance\; candidates working on adjacent topics are also welcome. LOCATION:
  London or Washington DC preferred\, but open to candidates based elsewher
 e (for example the Bay Area). ELIGIBILITY: open to a wide range of academi
 c and professional backgrounds (computer science\, political science\, pub
 lic policy\, economics\, philosophy\, law\, plus government/industry/civil
 -society experience)\; no strict educational requirement\, but the stronge
 st Fellow candidates typically have multiple years of relevant research or
  policy experience and a track record of high-quality\, impactful work. PR
 OCESS: written application\; a paid remote work test (some workstreams add
  a research agenda and a second paid test)\; then a final stage of two int
 erviews and reference checks. Deep expertise in technical governance\, fro
 ntier safety design\, cybersecurity or US policy is especially welcome. De
 adline 23:59 Anywhere on Earth\, Sunday 16 August 2026. Contact recruitmen
 t@governance.ai with location or access questions.\n\nOrganisation: Centre
  for the Governance of AI (GovAI)\nLocation: London\, UK or Washington\, D
 C preferred\; open to candidates based elsewhere. UK visa sponsorship avai
 lable\; US sponsorship only in exceptional cases.\nAward: Salary by experi
 ence\, role and location. Research Fellow: London GBP 84\,000-103\,500\; W
 ashington DC USD 134\,500-170\,000. Across both roles the combined range s
 tarts at GBP 75\,000 (London) / USD 100\,000 (DC)\, with the lower bounds 
 applying to the Research Scholar position. Plus pension or 401(k) contribu
 tion and benefits (health\, dental and vision insurance\; annual wellbeing
  and professional-development budgets\; coworking allowance for remote sta
 ff\; flexible hours\; extended parental leave\; 33 days paid vacation incl
 uding public holidays). No application fee.\nDuration: Research Fellow: 2-
 year full-time contract\, renewable. Research Scholar: 1-year visiting pos
 ition.\n\nMore: https://www.governance.ai/opportunities/research-fellow?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.governance.ai/opportunities/research-fellow?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:London\, UK or Washington\, DC preferred\; open to candidates base
 d elsewhere. UK visa sponsorship available\; US sponsorship only in except
 ional cases.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bloomberg-green-docs-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260814
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260815
SUMMARY:Deadline: Bloomberg Green Docs Competition 2026 (Short Climate Docu
 mentary)
DESCRIPTION:The Bloomberg Green Docs competition is open to all eligible fi
 lmmakers competing for a $25\,000 grand prize for a short climate document
 ary. The call seeks films that explore our climate future and reveal the w
 orld we are making today: ecosystems being restored\, tomorrow's zero-carb
 on communities being formed\, and habitat extremes warning of cascading cl
 imate consequences. REQUIREMENTS: films must address the urgency of climat
 e change and run under 10 minutes including credits. The jury awards a $25
 \,000 grand prize\, announced at the Bloomberg Green Docs Film Festival in
  New York City on 22 September 2026\, where the top five documentaries wil
 l be screened. Check the official rules on the competition site for eligib
 ility details. DEADLINE: submissions accepted through 14 August 2026.\n\nO
 rganisation: Bloomberg Green\nLocation: Open call for filmmakers (see elig
 ibility rules on the site)\; festival screening in New York City\, USA.\nA
 ward: $25\,000 grand prize awarded by a jury\; the top five documentaries 
 screen at the Bloomberg Green Docs Film Festival in New York City. No entr
 y fee stated.\nDuration: Winner announced at the festival on 22 September 
 2026\n\nMore: https://www.bloomberg.com/greendocs/?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.bloomberg.com/greendocs/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Open call for filmmakers (see eligibility rules on the site)\; fes
 tival screening in New York City\, USA.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:new-emergence-art-prize-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260803
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260804
SUMMARY:Deadline: New Emergence Art Prize 2026
DESCRIPTION:The New Emergence Art Prize 2026 is an international open call 
 for emerging artists\, presented by New Emergence Art. There is no set the
 me and work may be submitted in any medium. AWARDS: a prize fund worth ove
 r GBP 5\,000 across nine awards supporting artists through cash prizes\, a
 rtist materials\, mentoring\, and a residency\, including a GBP 1\,000 Gra
 nd Prize\, a two-week NG Art Creative Residency in Provence\, France (wort
 h EUR 2\,000)\, a GBP 300 Founder's Prize cash award\, and materials or su
 bscription awards from Staedtler UK\, Rosemary & Co\, GreatArt\, Michael H
 arding\, and The Art Ladder. ELIGIBILITY: open internationally to emerging
  artists\; entrants may submit up to three original artworks that represen
 t the quality\, originality and direction of their practice. Final selecti
 ons are made by an independent judging panel considering concept\, executi
 on\, originality and impact. Shortlisted artists are featured in an online
  exhibition running 11 August to 7 September 2026. NOTE: there is a paid e
 ntry fee (amount set at the entry step). DEADLINE: 3 August 2026\, 23:59 B
 ST. Apply via https://www.newemergenceart.com/apply.\n\nOrganisation: New 
 Emergence Art\nLocation: International\; open worldwide. Shortlist shown i
 n an online exhibition.\nAward: Prize fund worth over GBP 5\,000 across ni
 ne awards\, including a GBP 1\,000 Grand Prize cash award\, a two-week NG 
 Art Creative Residency in Provence\, France (worth EUR 2\,000)\, a GBP 300
  Founder's Prize cash award\, and materials or subscription awards from St
 aedtler UK\, Rosemary & Co\, GreatArt\, Michael Harding\, and The Art Ladd
 er. NOTE: there is a paid entry fee.\nDuration: One-off prize\; shortliste
 d artists shown in an online exhibition 11 August to 7 September 2026\n\nM
 ore: https://www.newemergenceart.com/apply?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.newemergenceart.com/apply?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:International\; open worldwide. Shortlist shown in an online exhib
 ition.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fondazione-prada-film-fund-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260910
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260911
SUMMARY:Deadline: Fondazione Prada Film Fund 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Fondazione Prada Film Fund is an annual initiative supporti
 ng independent cinema\, with an annual endowment of EUR 1.5 million backin
 g up to 14 feature film projects across development\, production\, and pos
 t-production. The Fund imposes NO restrictions on geography\, theme\, genr
 e\, or language and does not participate as a co-producer\, guaranteeing c
 omplete artistic freedom to filmmakers. It supports both established autho
 rs and new talents\, as well as research projects. WHICH TRACKS ARE STILL 
 OPEN: the 2026 call opened 30 June 2026 and runs on two separate deadlines
 . The projects-in-production track has already CLOSED (6 August 2026). Dev
 elopment and post-production remain open to 10 September 2026\, which is t
 he deadline this card now tracks. Post-production applicants can keep work
 ing after submitting: a rough cut may be supplied up to 20 November 2026. 
 SELECTION is based exclusively on quality\, originality\, vision\, and the
  strength and reliability of the applicant structure\; projects are evalua
 ted by independent readers and external consultants\, with final selection
  and grant amounts determined by a committee appointed by Fondazione Prada
  and composed of prominent figures from cinema and culture. No fixed per-s
 tage grant bands are published\, so the committee sets each award. ELIGIBI
 LITY: applications may be submitted only by a production company involved 
 in the project\, and that company remains solely responsible for meeting a
 ll requirements and conditions\; obligations of selected beneficiaries are
  governed by a contract with Fondazione Prada. Open worldwide\, with no ge
 ographic restriction at all. PHISHING WARNING FROM THE FUNDER: all officia
 l communications about the Film Fund come only from addresses in the fonda
 zioneprada.org domain. Treat anything from another domain as a probable fr
 aud or phishing attempt and verify before responding. The second edition's
  selected projects are announced February 2027. Application documents are 
 on the Fondazione Prada Film Fund page\; questions to filmfund@fondazionep
 rada.org.\n\nOrganisation: Fondazione Prada\nLocation: Milan\, Italy-based
  fund\; open worldwide to independent producers and production companies.\
 nAward: EUR 1.5 million annual endowment supporting up to 14 projects. Ind
 ividual grant amounts are set by the Fondazione Prada selection committee 
 (no fixed per-stage bands published). No application fee.\nDuration: Featu
 re film funding at development\, production or post-production stage. The 
 2026 call opened 30 June 2026. Projects in production closed 6 August 2026
 \; projects in development and post-production close 10 September 2026. Po
 st-production projects may supply a rough cut until 20 November 2026\, wit
 h selections announced February 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.fondazioneprada
 .org/film-fund/?lang=en&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
URL:https://www.fondazioneprada.org/film-fund/?lang=en&utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Milan\, Italy-based fund\; open worldwide to independent producers
  and production companies.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:liu-shiming-artist-grants-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Liu Shiming Art Foundation: Artist Grants 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Liu Shiming Art Foundation's annual Artist Grants (fourth e
 dition) award up to five grants of USD 5\,000 each to early-career visual 
 artists to create new work exploring connections between traditional\, cul
 tural\, or historical influences and contemporary artistic practice. ELIGI
 BILITY: visual artists in the first decade of their career (two to ten yea
 rs practicing professionally)\; applicants must have received a studio-art
 s degree between 2016 and 2024\, or had their debut gallery or juried-exhi
 bition show between 2016 and 2026. Open to artists of any country\; the ap
 plication and all supporting materials must be in English. TIMELINE: appli
 cations open 1 June 2026\; close 31 August 2026\; recipients notified by 2
  December 2026. Apply via the Foundation's grants page.\n\nOrganisation: L
 iu Shiming Art Foundation\nLocation: Open to artists of any country\; appl
 ication must be in English.\nAward: Up to five grants of USD 5\,000 each. 
 Disbursed in two payments (USD 3\,000 by 15 January 2027 and USD 2\,000 on
  project completion). No application fee.\nDuration: Project grant for new
  work\n\nMore: https://www.lsmartfund.org/grants?utm_source=artificialnouv
 eaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.lsmartfund.org/grants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk
 &utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Open to artists of any country\; application must be in English.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iias-asia-in-the-world-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260910
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260911
SUMMARY:Deadline: IIAS: Asia in the World Fellowship Programme 2027-2028
DESCRIPTION:The Asia in the World Fellowship Programme\, run by the Interna
 tional Institute for Asian Studies (IIAS) at Leiden University\, is a nine
 -month\, two-phase fellowship pairing a residency at a partner institution
  in Asia\, Africa\, Latin America\, or Europe (Phase 1) with six months in
  residence at IIAS in Leiden\, the Netherlands (Phase 2). The programme su
 pports interdisciplinary\, humanities- and social-science-based research e
 ngaging Asia and its connections with the wider world. SUPPORT during the 
 Leiden phase: EUR 1\,250 per month stipend\, EUR 1\,200 per month housing 
 allowance\, and a travel grant\; the Phase 1 stipend is provided by the co
 -host institution. ELIGIBILITY: scholars and researchers worldwide\; open 
 internationally. APPLICATIONS open 1 July 2026\; deadline 10 September 202
 6. Apply via the IIAS fellowships page. NOTE: dates confirmed via secondar
 y sources at time of entry (the funder page was not directly reachable)\; 
 verify before applying.\n\nOrganisation: International Institute for Asian
  Studies (IIAS)\, Leiden University\nLocation: Two phases: a partner insti
 tution in Asia\, Africa\, Latin America\, or Europe (Phase 1)\, then Leide
 n\, Netherlands (Phase 2). Open internationally.\nAward: Leiden phase: EUR
  1\,250 per month stipend plus EUR 1\,200 per month housing allowance\, pl
 us a travel grant\; the Phase 1 stipend at the co-host institution is set 
 by that institution. No application fee.\nDuration: 9 months (two phases)\
 n\nMore: https://www.iias.asia/fellowships/asia-in-the-world?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.iias.asia/fellowships/asia-in-the-world?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Two phases: a partner institution in Asia\, Africa\, Latin America
 \, or Europe (Phase 1)\, then Leiden\, Netherlands (Phase 2). Open interna
 tionally.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:aixdesign-grain-cookbook-critical-ai-literacy-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T203645Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: AIxDESIGN: Grain\, Open Call for Critical AI Literacy Rec
 ipes
DESCRIPTION:Grain is a cookbook of critical and creative AI literacy exerci
 ses for educators in film and the wider audiovisual and creative field\, c
 o-produced by AIxDESIGN and the Netherlands Film Academy and led by Nadia 
 Piet. It publishes in March/April 2027\, digital and print\, under CC BY-S
 A 4.0. The current open call is to propose a recipe by the end of August 2
 026 and join as a launch contributor. WHAT THEY WANT: tried-and-tested gro
 up exercises you have actually run with people in teaching or workshops\, 
 specifically in the context of film\, audiovisual work\, time-based media 
 and storytelling. Hands-on or analog\, 15 minutes or a full day\, beginner
  or advanced\; the one hard requirement is that another educator can read 
 your recipe and run it themselves. What that might look like: hands-on exp
 eriments with specific AI tools (image generators\, chatbots\, code assist
 ants\, video models)\; analog activities that reveal computational logic t
 hrough embodied practice\; critical readings\, watchings or listenings wit
 h structured discussion\; provocations\, games or role-plays that get at h
 ow AI sees\, thinks or makes\; and exercises that help people work out how
  they want to relate to AI in their own practice. WHAT MAKES A GOOD RECIPE
 : it helps a room of people think together and trusts the room to be smart
  given the right container\; it emerges from real practice rather than an 
 untested idea\; it is specific about what participants do\, step by step\,
  so a stranger could pick it up and adapt it to their group\, time\, tools
  or context\; it has at least one designed social moment where the thinkin
 g happens between people rather than privately\; it is about something lar
 ger than technique (how a machine sees an image\, where your values bump u
 p against what the tools want from you\, what a film's rhythm looks like l
 aid out in space instead of time)\; it takes a position without telling pe
 ople what to think\; and it points beyond itself with references\, reading
  and reference projects. Published example recipes include Image Forgery: 
 Archival Mimesis with AI (Artefacto)\, Machine Ways of Seeing (Nadia Piet)
 \, AI Film Now: A Close Watching Exercise (Nadia Piet) and Outside the Tim
 eline: Spatial Grid + Visual Clustering (Artefacto)\, alongside a root rec
 ipe template. WHAT LAUNCH CONTRIBUTORS GET: your recipe in the first editi
 on\, print and online\, credited to you\; a print copy when it lands and a
 n invite to the launch event in Amsterdam in spring 2027\; support turning
  your exercise into a recipe\, via Recipe Press (their LLM helper)\, the c
 ook-along\, or directly with the team\; and a need-based fee of EUR 150 pe
 r recipe for freelance and independent practitioners\, which you request i
 n the proposal form. After this round contributions stay open but web-only
 : no fee\, no dedicated support\, no print. COOK-ALONG: one live 30-minute
  online session on Wednesday 12 August 2026 at 16:00 CEST\, where you brin
 g a rough exercise and turn it into a recipe on the spot with Recipe Press
  (sign up at luma.com/84w148in). HOW IT WORKS: (1) propose your recipe thr
 ough the proposal form (who you are\, what the exercise is\, where you hav
 e run it\, and whether you are requesting the fee)\; a short pitch is enou
 gh\, and if it is already written you can just link your draft\; (2) they 
 reply on a rolling basis\, with one reply covering both whether it is a ma
 tch for the first edition and the fee if you requested it\, so the earlier
  you send the earlier you hear back\; (3) once it is a yes\, write it up u
 sing the structure on the Contribute a Recipe page\, with Recipe Press or 
 at the cook-along. TIMELINE: proposals open until the end of August 2026\,
  reviewed as they come in\; accepted recipes due in full by mid-September 
 2026\; Grain publishes March/April 2027. Questions to hello@aixdesign.co.\
 n\nOrganisation: AIxDESIGN\, co-produced with the Netherlands Film Academy
 \nLocation: Remote/online\; open to educators and practitioners based anyw
 here\nAward: Need-based fee of EUR 150 per recipe for freelance and indepe
 ndent practitioners: say so in the proposal form if making the time would 
 be hard without it\, and it is settled in the same reply. Launch contribut
 ors also get their recipe in the first edition of Grain in print and onlin
 e\, credited to them\, a print copy when it lands\, an invite to the launc
 h event in Amsterdam in spring 2027\, and support turning the exercise int
 o a recipe. Published under CC BY-SA 4.0\; you keep authorship. No applica
 tion fee.\nDuration: One-off contribution: proposals by end of August 2026
 \, accepted recipes due in full by mid-September 2026\; Grain publishes Ma
 rch/April 2027\n\nMore: https://nadiapiet.notion.site/Open-Call-Grain-Cook
 book-for-Critical-AI-Literacy-38e37d003833804380acd027b6041d5a?utm_source=
 artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://nadiapiet.notion.site/Open-Call-Grain-Cookbook-for-Critical-AI-
 Literacy-38e37d003833804380acd027b6041d5a?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote/online\; open to educators and practitioners based anywhere
END:VEVENT
